<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nativity of Jesus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 6–4 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Also known as The Nativity, Birth of Jesus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Nativity of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> is the birth of Jesus as told in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">Gospels</a> of Matthew and Luke, the basis of the Christian holiday of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a>. The two accounts agree that Jesus was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bethlehem">Bethlehem</a> in Judea in the reign of King Herod, to Mary, whose husband Joseph was descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">King David</a>, and that the conception was divinely effected. Only Matthew has the wise men, the star of Bethlehem, the Massacre of the Innocents and the flight into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>; only Luke has the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/publius-sulpicius-quirinius">Quirinius</a> and the adoration of the shepherds. The gospels state no date; reckoning from the death of Herod would place the birth between 6 and 4 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nativity-of-jesus.jpg" alt="Nativity of Jesus" width="330" height="223" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Paris Orlando · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Adoration_of_the_sheperds_-_Matthias_Stomer.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Nativity</b> is told in Matthew and Luke, which agree that Jesus was born in Bethlehem in Judea in the reign of King Herod; reckoning from the death of Herod would place the birth between 6 and 4 BCE, but neither account names a day.</blockquote><a href="/essays/christmas-day-in-history">Christmas Day in History</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Nativity</b> is told by Matthew and Luke, who agree that he was born in Bethlehem in the reign of King Herod, to Mary, and that the conception was divinely effected.</blockquote><a href="/essays/midwinter">Midwinter</a></li><li><blockquote>It draws on the <b>Nativity of Jesus</b> and reflects his social and abolitionist views.</blockquote><a href="/songs/o-holy-night">O Holy Night</a></li></ul></section>
